1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:04,680
Um, imagine your minister just announced this, uh, streamlined new digital service.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,220
Right? Handshakes happen, the-

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,620
Press releases go out.

4
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:13,340
Exactly. But, like, a year later, the software launches, and it does something completely different than what was promised.

5
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:19,260
And if you're the official running this program in your ministry, well, you're the one who gets hauled in to explain why.

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:20,320
Yeah, it is, um-

7
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:22,040
It's a really painful position to be in.

8
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,140
Right. So today we're doing a deep dive to solve this exact mystery.

9
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:31,180
Like, why does official policy so often get lost in translation by the time it becomes code?

10
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:35,400
And the reference architecture gives us a really precise framework for this.

11
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,780
It's called specification driven development, or SDD,

12
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:44,000
and it explains exactly where these disconnects happen and, you know, how you can stop them.

13
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,120
Okay, so let's unpack this. If the minister didn't change their mind,

14
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:54,280
um, what exactly happens in that wide space between an official policy agreement and the final running system?

15
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:58,300
Well, what happens is that government departments are incredibly siloed.

16
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:02,780
I mean, the team writing the policy assumes that developers know exactly how to implement it.

17
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:04,239
Right, which they don't.

18
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:10,420
Exactly. And the developers assume the policy team has, you know, thought through every single edge case.

19
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:18,260
So between what officials agree on and what a system actually does, there are hundreds of minute unanswered questions.

20
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:19,360
Like what?

21
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:26,900
Like, uh, what happens when an applicant leaves a document out, or which department actually owns the applicant's address data.

22
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:34,860
Wait, so if nobody explicitly wrote those answers down in the policy, does a random developer just, like, invent government policy at

23
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:38,620
two PM on a Tuesday just because they have a blank data field to fill?

24
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:39,680
They really do.

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,780
I mean, every single question must be answered for the code to run.

26
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:51,300
So if leaders don't force those questions into the open, they are answered quietly by whoever happens to be at the keyboard that day.

27
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:53,100
Just to keep the project moving.

28
00:01:53,560 --> 00:02:00,460
Exactly. And there's no official record of it, so the service gets delivered, but it's, uh, it's fragmented.

29
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:03,300
It doesn't do what anyone actually agreed it would do.

30
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:04,100
Right.

31
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:11,180
So to catch these, like, silent decisions before they happen, the SDD method highlights three specific points of failure.

32
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:11,780
Yes.

33
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:15,420
The first is a question nobody decided.

34
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:21,400
Because departments didn't want to hold up production to make a formal decision, so the builders just had to guess.

35
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,440
Precisely. The policymakers never actually settled the detail.

36
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:32,000
It's like, uh, building a house, right? The bricks aren't bad, but nobody handed the plumber the blueprints.

37
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:33,460
That's a perfect analogy.

38
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:36,840
So they just kind of guessed where the pipes should go.

39
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:38,200
Okay, what's the second?

40
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:40,980
Number two is an answer nobody passed on.

41
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:49,860
So somebody did make the decision, but because they're so siloed, that decision never reached the technical document or the specific programmer who needed it.

42
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:50,800
Oh, okay.

43
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:51,960
And the third?

44
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:55,200
Number three is a rule nobody enforced.

45
00:02:55,860 --> 00:03:02,400
So a written rule existed in a strategy somewhere, but there was no check or review process to actually hold anyone to it.

46
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:08,620
Uh, let's bring this to life a bit. The reference architecture uses a fictional country called Progressa to show how this plays out.

47
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:09,140
Right.

48
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:15,640
So in Progressa, let's say Harbor View University College registers with PHEQA.

49
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:17,180
Uh, that's the quality authority.

50
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:21,580
Okay. And they get entered into the official register under a specific ID number.

51
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:25,360
Right. But a few months later, that same college moves offices.

52
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:30,960
They tell PHEQA about the address change, but then MoEYS, the Ministry of Education,

53
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:36,040
asks the college to fill out a brand-new form with the exact same particulars.

54
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:37,820
Because they didn't talk to PHEQA.

55
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:39,800
So the college submits the new form.

56
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:43,420
And then two weeks later, the Ministry of Education flags a discrepancy.

57
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:48,060
Wow. So they ask the college why their address doesn't match the one on file.

58
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:53,560
Yep. The work is completely rekeyed and duplicated, and, you know, it's costly to change.

59
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:59,280
Even though the national strategy literally said an institution should only have to enter a fact once.

60
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:02,800
So let's map the situation back to the three SDD failures.

61
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:08,240
First, nobody ever decided whether the Ministry of Education was even legally allowed to read PHEQA's register.

62
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:08,420
Right?

63
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:08,920
Right.

64
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,600
So the ministry just built its own form.

65
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:14,140
That's number one, a question nobody decided.

66
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:19,000
Second, PHEQA never actually shared that ID number system with the ministry.

67
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:21,140
So they couldn't look the college up anyway.

68
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:22,740
That's an answer nobody passed on.

69
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,980
You got it. And third, that rule to enter a fact once,

70
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:32,540
it was a mandate on paper, but no technical review ever tested the software to enforce it.

71
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,360
A rule nobody enforced.

72
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:37,840
You know, what I find so striking about this is

73
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:40,720
none of these failures were programming errors.

74
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:42,560
Like, the code worked perfectly fine.

75
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:43,620
Precisely.

76
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:49,900
Each loss was a decision about the service that was just never made, never communicated, or never checked.

77
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:54,120
A silence that is named and owned is a specification,

78
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,620
but a silence filled in with a guessed answer is a fault.

79
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:03,480
So if you want to fix a fragmented digital service, you have to find out which of those three places failed.

